  "statement": {
    "representation": "pdf-text-entry",
    "text": "a) Can the free Burnside group B(m, n), for any m and n, be given by defining\nrelations of the form v n = 1 such that for any natural divisor d of n distinct from n\nthe element v d is not trivial in B(m, n)? This is true for odd n ⩾ 665, and for all\nn ⩾ 248 divisible by 29 .                                                  S. V. Ivanov",
